{# TM·03 — THE ORIENTATION WIDGET, and the note without which every list above
   is read wrongly.

   Every list on this page shows a tier beside a position and the two look like
   one scale. They are two mechanisms with two enforcement points, and since the
   Manager tier was removed the difference has become sharper rather than
   subtler: the tier column no longer tells you who administers this
   installation, and team.manage does. #}
{% import '@UhifadhiTeam/team/_bits.html.twig' as bits %}

<div class="c" data-tm>
    <span class="tab"><span class="idx">TM&middot;03</span>How authority works here<span class="src">&middot; read this before the list above</span></span>

    <div class="tm-two">
        <div>
            <span class="lbl">Tier &mdash; two escape hatches, and everyone else</span>
            <p class="use" style="margin:0">Set on the person&rsquo;s own account, and deliberately <b>almost never
            used</b>. The top two tiers exist so an installation can be bootstrapped and recovered; they are not
            how authority is meant to be handed out day to day.</p>
            <div class="tm-gate">
                {% for tier in tiers %}
                    <div class="tm-gaterow">
                        <span class="gk">{{ bits.tier_pill(tier) }}</span>
                        <span class="gv">{{ tier.description }}</span>
                    </div>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        </div>
        <div>
            <span class="lbl">Permission &mdash; gates capability, including authority over people</span>
            <p class="use" style="margin:0">Held by a <b>position</b>, never by a person directly. A person&rsquo;s
            capabilities are their position&rsquo;s permission list, and there is no per-person override anywhere
            in the model.</p>
            <div class="tm-gate">
                <div class="tm-gaterow">
                    <span class="gk">Where it lives</span>
                    <span class="gv"><code>team_position.permissions</code> &mdash; a JSON list of strings, one row per position, and a position belongs to a department.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="tm-gaterow">
                    <span class="gk">Who checks it</span>
                    <span class="gv"><code>PermissionVoter</code>: grant if the tier is Super Admin or Admin, else <code>position?-&gt;hasPermissionValue()</code>.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="tm-gaterow">
                    <span class="gk"><span class="tm-mgr">{{ bits.icon_shield() }}team.manage</span></span>
                    <span class="gv"><b>This is what makes somebody an administrator.</b> {{ teamManage.description }}</span>
                </div>
                <div class="tm-gaterow">
                    <span class="gk">No position</span>
                    <span class="gv">No capabilities. Not &ldquo;the defaults&rdquo; and not &ldquo;read only&rdquo; &mdash; <b>none</b>.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="tm-gaterow">
                    <span class="gk">Where to change it</span>
                    <span class="gv"><a href="{{ path('team_positions') }}">Positions &amp; permissions</a> &mdash; you change a position, and everyone holding it moves.</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tm-correct">
        {{ bits.icon_info() }}
        <div><b>There is no Manager tier, and removing it was the improvement.</b> Administering the team used to
        be a rung on the tier ladder &mdash; a second authority system running invisibly beside the matrix, which
        nobody could grant, revoke or audit from the one screen built for exactly that. It is now
        <code>team.manage</code>: an ordinary row in the catalogue, under the <b>Team</b> umbrella, granted by a
        position like everything else. <b>&ldquo;A manager&rdquo; is now a position somebody composed</b> &mdash;
        and it can have the row taken away this afternoon without touching anybody&rsquo;s account.</div>
    </div>
</div>
